WHAT HAPPENED. Chasing the QuotE failure on 2026-09-07, Tony gated the old parse
on hasTraits instead of hasAttribute. The result was a major face plant: NOTHING
PARSED. It was backed out the same night and the real fix landed elsewhere --
testAttributes now tolerates an attribute list holding only a noPrint entry,
which is commit 0150f29.

WHAT THIS INCANTATION MEASURES, and it is not what the banking assumed. Over the
66 members of Grokking, read from inside an action on the member and captured
with :=, on a grammar that has NOT been through setParse:

    HT-1  rules seen                66
    HT-2  carrying hasAttributeS    42
    HT-3  carrying hasTraitS        42
    HT-4  carrying BOTH             42
    HT-5  the two flags DISAGREE     0

⚠ SO THE FLAGS AGREE EXACTLY, HERE. The guess this was banked with -- "a writer
bypasses addAttribute, so the flag is stale" -- IS NOT SUPPORTED BY THIS
MEASUREMENT and is withdrawn as stated. Whatever made the gate face-plant, it is
not a population difference on the untouched grammar.

⚠ HT-4 IS NOT DECORATION. HT-5 wants 0, and a run where the probe read nothing
at all would also print 0. HT-4 at 42 is what makes HT-5's zero mean "they
agree" rather than "nothing was measured".

WHERE THE DIVERGENCE IS, and it is the live thread. connectiveT pins StatemenT at
hasAttributeS 1 / hasTraitS 0 and that row is RED today, reading 1/1. So the two
flags DO come apart -- after setParse, or after whatever connectiveT does, and
not on the raw grammar. That red fleet row is this file's other half.

NEXT: OPEN.

No fix is recommended. The guess that came with the banking has been measured and
withdrawn; do not re-infer it from this file.

WHAT WOULD GENERATE ONE, cheapest first:
  1. Run this same census AFTER setParse and diff the five numbers. If HT-5 goes
     non-zero there, the divergence is setParse's and the population that moved
     is named in one run. That is the measurement this file was one step short of.
  2. Read updateContentFlags against the direct writers of hasTraits. If the
     recompute and the direct writes disagree about what a trait IS, that is the
     answer and it is one read.
  3. Only then decide: repair, retire, or document. RETIRING IS A LIVE OPTION --
     a flag with no reader and no maintainer is not obviously worth fixing, and
     the old parse is blind to the new parse, so there is no customer today.

⚠ TWO SPELLING TRAPS PAID FOR WHILE WRITING THIS, both worth the line:
  . htProbe(BlocK) CRASHED THE FILE at exit 139 with zero bytes. Naming a rule
    FIRES its action (bear-trap 34), and BlocK's action executes a block. The
    walk over Grokking members is not a stylistic choice; passing a rule by name
    is not safe.
  . `if htA != htB;` counted 66 disagreements while HT-2 and HT-3 both read 42 --
    arithmetically impossible. `!=` on two captured flag locals is void. And
    `if htA == 1;` reads 0 for all 66, because the flag's stored value is a
    bitfield and not literally 1. The working shape is BARE for the flag
    (truthOf answers it) and == only on a derived counter.
